          <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="details" id="faq-1" hidden>
          <div class="details-inner">
            <div class="details-content">
              <p>
                Cats often purr when they feel safe and content, but purring can also be a way to
                self-soothe during stress or recovery. It is a versatile sound that may help with
                communication and comfort.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">
          <span>How much do cats sleep?</span>
          <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="details" id="faq-2" hidden>
          <div class="details-inner">
            <div class="details-content">
              <p>
                Most cats sleep between 12 and 16 hours a day, and some nap even longer. Their
                bursts of energy are balanced by lots of rest, especially for kittens and older cats.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">
          <span>Why do cats knead blankets or laps?</span>
          <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="details" id="faq-3" hidden>
          <div class="details-inner">
            <div class="details-content">
              <p>
                Kneading is a behavior cats learn as kittens and often keep into adulthood. It can
                signal relaxation, scent-marking, or simply that a soft spot feels especially cozy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">
          <span>Do indoor cats need enrichment?</span>
          <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="details" id="faq-4" hidden>
          <div class="details-inner">
            <div class="details-content">
              <p>
                Yes. Scratching posts, climbing spaces, puzzle feeders, window perches, and regular
                play sessions help indoor cats stay mentally engaged and physically active.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">
          <span>Why do cats flick their tails?</span>
          <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="details" id="faq-5" hidden>
          <div class="details-inner">
            <div class="details-content">
              <p>
                Tail movement can reveal mood. A gentle upright tail may show confidence or greeting,
                while a fast flick can mean irritation, intense focus, or overstimulation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script>
    const items = document.querySelectorAll(".example");

    items.forEach((item) => {
      const button = item.querySelector(".question");
      const details = item.querySelector(".details");

      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const isOpen = button.getAttribute("aria-expanded") === "true";

        button.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(!isOpen));
        item.classList.toggle("open", !isOpen);

        if (!isOpen) {
